          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Intuitionistic logic
            was introduced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arend+Heyting"} Arend Heyting
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            for
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer"} Brouwer
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            and so may also be called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong constructive logic
          %p
            Beware the terminological ambiguity: Some people insist that “intuitionistic logic” refers to Brouwerian intuitionism, which includes axioms that contradict classical logic; but other people use “intuitionistic” to mean the same as what in other contexts is called “constructive”, i.e. mathematics without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            but nothing added that contradicts them. Some people (particularly material set theorists) use “constructive” to mean
            %em predicative
            constructive and “intuitionistic” to mean impredicative constructive.
          %h2#idea_2 Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            Intuitionistic logic is most easily described as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            without the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash A \vee \neg{A}
            ) or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double-negation"} double-negation
            rule (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg{A} \vdash A
            ). It may also be defined by starting with Gentzen's
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg
            but not
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash \Delta
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            may contain
            %em at most one
            formula, or by starting with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
            and restricting to such sequents where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            must contain
            %em exactly one
            formula.
          %h3#ConstructiveInterpretationOfConnectives Constructive interpretation of connectives
          %p
            The
            %em intuitionistic interpretation
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+connectives"} logical connectives
            (known as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Heyting56"} Heyting (1956, §7.1.1)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, §2)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            is regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            only if it is possible to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} construct
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/assertion"} assertion
          %p
            For instance, to assert a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} logical conjunction
            (“and”) or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            (“for all”) is taken to mean to provide a proof of all the instances.
          %p
            Dually but more notably, to assert a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+disjunction"} logical disjunction
            (“or”) or an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
            (“exists”) is taken to mean to prove one of the instances, so that there is no intuitionistic existence statement without construction of an example (the “disjunction property”, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#DisjunctionProperty"} below
          %p
            This constructive interpretation of logical truth is the crux of the rejection of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+excluded+middle"} principle of excluded middle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \vee (\not P)
            (which may superficially/classically seem tautologous) one must prove
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or one must prove
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \not P
            — but neither proof may be known (e.g. if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            \=
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
          %p (Here the classical mathematician is regarded as “idealistic” in their assumption that either case must hold, even if it is impossible to tell which one.)
          %p#FromKolmogorov
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Kolmogorov32"} Kolmogorov (1932, p. 59)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/KolmogorovIntroducingBHK.jpg", :width => "550px"}/
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Heyting56"} Heyting (1956, p. 97)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HeytingIntroducingBHK.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra69"} Troelstra (1969, p. 5)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Troelstra-IntroducingBHKInterp.jpg", :width => "660px"}/
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Troelstra77"} Troelstra (1977, p. 977)
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Troelstra-AttributingBHK.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p#FromBridges
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Bridges99"} Bridges (1999), p. 96
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Bridges-IntuitInterpOfConnectives.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            The analogous discussion for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            is then given spring in
            %a{:href => "#Girard89"} Girard (1989, §2)
            and with more emphasis in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#MartinLöf96"} Martin-Löf (1996, Lec 3)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#DoubleNegation Double negation
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation+translation"} double negation translation
            says that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is provable in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            precisely if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double negation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \not \not P
            is provable in constructive logic.
          %h3#DisjunctionProperty Disjunction property
          %p
            Unlike classical logic, intuitionistic logic has the
            %em disjunction-
            and
            %em existence
            properties (cf.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#ConstructiveInterpretationOfConnectives"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash A \vee B
            must contain a proof of either
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash A
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash \exists x.\,F(x)
            must construct a term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            and a proof of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash F(t)
          %p
            On the other hand, (classical)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            is conservative over (intuitionistic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+arithmetic"} Heyting arithmetic
            when restricted to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^0_1
            formulas; that is, formulas of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi N
                    %mo .
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi N
                    %mo .
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x\colon N.\, \exists y\colon N.\, F(x,y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
            is concerned.
          %h2#classicality_principles Classicality principles
          %p
            The principle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            is not provable in intuitionistic logic, and if we assume it then the logic becomes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          %ul
            %li
              various
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omniscience+principles"} omniscience principles
              such as LPO and LLPO
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double-negation+shift"} double-negation shift
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan%27s+law"}> de Morgan's law
              , i.e.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+excluded+middle"} weak excluded middle
            %li
              the
              %span.newWikiWord
                world's simplest axiom of choice
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/world%27s+simplest+axiom+of+choice"} ?
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BHK+interpretation"} BHK interpretation
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                  %th
